\label{prop:1}
Let $P\subset\Bbb R^3$ be a parallelepiped containing five vertices of $Q_3$.
Then $P+\Bbb Z^3=\Bbb R^3$.

\label{cor:3}
A pyramid in $Q_n$ with base an $(n-1)$-dimensional parallelotope has a volume
at most $1/n$.

\label{lem:5}
A simplex $\sigma$ can be translated into a parallelotope $P$ if and only if
for every pair $u,v$ of vertices of $\sigma$ we have $u-v\in P-P$.

Write the vertices of $Q_3$ as $ijk$, where $i,j,k\in\{0,1\}$, and set
$A=000$, $B=100$, $C=110$, $D=010$, $A_1=001$,
$B_1=101$, $C_1=111$, and $D_1=011$.
Up to symmetries of the cube, there are three configurations of five vertices:

\noindent(i) $A,B,C,D,A_1$;\quad (ii) $A,B,D,A_1,C_1$;\quad (iii) $A,B,C,D_1,C_1$.
\smallskip

(i) The covering follows from Theorem~\ref{thm:2}.

\begin{figure}[H]
\caption{Two triangulations of a triangular prism.
The added diagonals are shown in red.}
\label{fig:1}
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[
    scale=1.1,
    edge/.style={
        draw=black!65,
        line width=0.9pt
    },
    diagonal/.style={
        draw=red!70,
        line width=1.2pt
    },
    hidden/.style={
        dash pattern=on 4pt off 3pt
    },
    vertex/.style={
        circle,
        draw=black,
        fill=blue!70,
        line width=0.5pt,
        inner sep=0pt,
        minimum size=4pt
    },
    every node/.style={font=\large}
]


\foreach \offset/\variant in {0/1, 5.5/2}{
    \begin{scope}[xshift=\offset cm]

        \coordinate (A) at (0,0);
        \coordinate (B) at (3,-1.2);
        \coordinate (C) at (3.6,0);


        \coordinate (A1) at (0,3);
        \coordinate (B1) at (3,1.8);
        \coordinate (C1) at (3.6,3);


        \draw[edge] (A)--(B)--(C);
        \draw[edge,hidden] (A)--(C);
        \draw[edge] (A1)--(B1)--(C1)--cycle;
        \draw[edge] (A)--(A1)
                    (B)--(B1)
                    (C)--(C1);


        \draw[diagonal,hidden] (A)--(C1);
        \ifnum\variant=1\relax
            \draw[diagonal] (A)--(B1)--(C);
        \else
            \draw[diagonal] (A1)--(B)--(C1);
        \fi


        \foreach \point in {A,B,C,A1,B1,C1}{
            \node[vertex] at (\point) {};
        }


        \node[below left] at (A) {$A$};
        \node[below left] at (B) {$B$};
        \node[below, xshift=3pt] at (C) {$C$};
        \node[above left] at (A1) {$A_1$};
        \node[below left, yshift=-3pt, xshift=3pt]
            at (B1) {$B_1$};
        \node[above] at (C1) {$C_1$};
    \end{scope}
}
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{figure}
(ii) We triangulate the prism $ABCA_1B_1C_1$ using the edges
$AC_1, AB_1, B_1C$. The prism $ADCA_1D_1C_1$ is triangulated by translating $AB_1$
and $B_1C$ onto the opposite facets.
\smallskip

(iii) We triangulate the prism $ABCA_1B_1C_1$ using the edges
$AC_1, BA_1, BC_1$. The prism $ADCA_1D_1C_1$ is triangulated by translating $BA_1$
and $BC_1$ onto the opposite facets.

In both cases, (ii) and (iii), the triangulations induced on opposite facets
of $Q_3$ agree under translation. The resulting triangulation therefore extends
periodically to $\Bbb R^3$ and has vertex set $\Bbb Z^3$. Moreover, the difference
of the endpoints of every edge is, up to sign, the difference of two of the five
selected vertices. Hence the covering follows from
Lemmas~\ref{lem:5} and~\ref{lem:4}.
